WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:07.440
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.740
We continue discussion
of nuclear decays

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.820
with gamma decays.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:16.260
We have seen that we can
understand nuclear stability

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:17.670
or instability.

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:20.550
We discussed alpha
decays and beta decays.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:23.400
Now after the discussion
of the shell model,

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:29.730
it is apparent that transition
from various nuclear states

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:33.575
can be accomplished via the
admission of a photon, a gamma

00:00:33.575 --> 00:00:35.340
ray.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:40.740
Gamma decays are specifically
important in decay chains

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.680
following an alpha
decay or a beta decay

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.630
where the remnant,
the daughter nuclei,

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:48.780
is left over in
an excited state.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:54.120
Then the de-excitation follows
with the admission of a photon.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.670
Practical consequences
of this alpha example--

00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:00.930
in fission processes where a
significant amount of energies

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:04.530
can be released with
photons, radiotherapy

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:08.430
where we try to
remove cancer cells

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:12.870
or kill cancer cells
with gamma rays.

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:15.930
Medical imaging works this way.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:19.710
And in general, you can use
the emission of those photons

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:23.130
to deduce the spin and the
parity of excited states.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:26.110
If you go back very
early to this lecture,

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:28.470
we discussed the Wu experiment.

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:31.290
And also there we
used gamma rays

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:36.730
in order to reduce the spin
and the parity of the states

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:37.230
involved.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:43.760
So nuclear spectroscopy, we
haven't discussed the detectors

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:44.260
yet.

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:48.590
But what's shown
here in this picture

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:55.740
are two characteristic
gamma ray spectra

00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:00.000
from the case of
cobalt and cesium.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.060
And if you just focus on
the blue line, for example,

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:06.000
you see here this peak.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.800
This corresponds to the
energy of a transition.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.380
But then photons,
when they're emitted,

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:14.210
they can go through
Compton scattering.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:15.920
And they can lose
through Compton

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:17.310
scattering some of the energy.

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:19.290
So you see this tail here.

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:22.338
And then in this tail
you see additional peaks.

00:02:22.338 --> 00:02:24.380
And those additional peaks
can come from the fact

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:31.280
that a photon can produce
an electron-positron pair.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:33.590
And then you see one
or two of those pairs

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:38.270
in cases where one electron
or positron, or both of them,

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:40.680
have escaped the detection.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.370
So whenever you look
at a nuclear decay,

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:45.170
you find spectrums of the sort.

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:50.900
And then there are various
Compton scattering--

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.610
depends obviously
on the material

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:55.340
around and the
composition and also

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:58.460
this single and double
escape kind of peaks,

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:02.010
quite characteristic for the
material you're looking at.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:05.480
So from this, on
nuclear spectroscopy

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:08.360
you can learn about the sample
composition, the element

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:12.836
composition of the probe.

00:03:12.836 --> 00:03:16.820
And interesting effect
is the Mössbauer effect.

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:18.560
Here again, I'll
try to remind you

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:21.330
of the discussion of
special relativity.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:26.450
We looked at the energy of an
emitted and an absorbed photon.

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:35.450
And because in this emission
process the leftover nuclei,

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:36.960
there has to be a
momentum balance.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:40.620
So there is a recoil
on the leftover nuclei.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.290
So that means it starts moving.

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:45.650
And because it's moving, we
have to do a Doppler correction

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:50.450
of the energy, meaning that the
emitted photon energy is not

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:53.510
equal to the energy
needed in order to excite

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:56.340
the nuclear state again.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:58.310
So this leads then to
those energy spectrum.

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:00.170
Here, they're the
natural widths.

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:05.060
And only in this overlapping
region here you can reabsorb.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:10.340
Now the most Mössbauer effect
now is a special variation

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:12.380
of what I just described.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:19.790
In cases where the nucleon
is part of a lattice,

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:23.480
the lattice can absorb
the recoiling energy.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:28.700
And it leads like to a situation
of very, very heavy objects

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:30.030
absorbing this recoil.

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:33.290
And in those cases, you can
have resonant effects, meaning

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:35.840
that this emission line,
then absorption lines,

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:39.820
they lay over each
other quite strongly.

